1.1.text:
Article 22

(1) Regional Government conducts prevention as referred to in Article 21 letter a by:

a. socializing laws and regulations related to the implementation of protection of women and children;
b. providing counseling/guidance;
c. providing education about the dangers of domestic violence; d. establishing cooperation networks in efforts to prevent violence;
e. conducting coordination, integration, and synchronization of violence prevention based on partnership patterns;
f. collecting data and information and establishing a violence prevention system, mapping locations or areas prone to violence; g. conducting monitoring and evaluation of the implementation of protection of women and children;
h. increasing public awareness in behaving in accordance with social norms.
(2) In addition to prevention efforts carried out by the Regional Government as referred to in paragraph (1), prevention efforts are also carried out by families and/or the community.
(3) Prevention as referred to in paragraph (1) is organized in an integrated manner by PPT

Article 41

Every person is prohibited from:

a. treating women and children in a discriminatory manner which results in women and children experiencing loss, both material and moral, thus impeding their social function;
b. treating women and children with disabilities in a discriminatory manner;
c. placing, allowing, involving, ordering the involvement of children in situations of maltreatment and neglect;
d. placing, allowing, committing, ordering to commit or participating in committing violence against women and children;
e. committing violence or threats of violence to force women and children to have sexual intercourse with him or with another person;
f. committing violence or threats of violence, forcing, committing fraud, committing a series of lies, or persuading women and children to commit or allow obscene acts to be committed;
g. placing, allowing, committing, ordering to commit or participating in committing kidnapping, sale and/or trafficking of women and children; h. obstructing women and children from enjoying their own culture, acknowledging and practicing the teachings of their religion and/or using their own language without neglecting access to community and cultural development;
i. placing, allowing, committing, ordering to commit or participating in committing economic and/or sexual exploitation of women and children;
j. intentionally placing, allowing, involving women and children in the abuse, production and distribution of narcotics and/or psychotropic substances and/or
k. intentionally placing, allowing, involving, ordering the involvement of women and children in the abuse, production and distribution of alcohol and other addictive substances.

Article 3

The implementation of protection of women and children aims to: a. ensure the fulfillment of the rights of women and children so that they can participate optimally in society in accordance with human dignity; b. provide protection and provide a sense of security to women and children from all forms of violence and discrimination;

c. prevent and eliminate all forms of violence and exploitation against women and children including trafficking in persons;

d. provide services to women and children victims of violence, reporters, and witnesses; and

e. empower women and children victims of violence.

Article 32

(1) The community may participate in the implementation of protection of women and children.

(2) Community participation as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be in the form of: a. establishing family partners at the sub-district/village level;

b. establishing non-governmental organizations whose field of work is in the field of protection of women and children;

c. conducting socialization of women's rights independently;

d. conducting preventive efforts if finding indications of violence against women and children in the community;

e. providing first aid to women and children victims of violence; and

f. reporting to competent authorities if violence against women and children occurs in their environment.

(3) Community participation as referred to in paragraph (1) may be conducted by individuals, non-governmental organizations, educational institutions, religious institutions, and business entities.

Article 5

In implementing the protection of women and children, the Regional Government is obligated to:

a. establish basic policies for the implementation of protection of women and children in the Regional Action Plan;
b. implement policies for the protection of women and children established by the government and provincial government;
c. coordinate the implementation of policies, programs, and activities for the protection of the rights of women and children among regional apparatus organizations;
d. cooperate with other regions for the implementation of policies, programs, and activities for the protection of the rights of women and children;
e. strengthen the institutional mainstreaming of Gender and mainstreaming of children's rights for the implementation of policies, programs, and activities for the protection of the rights of women and children
f. establish PPT in the Region;
g. ensure the implementation of excellent service in the protection of women and children, including for women and children victims of violence; and
h. prepare a data system for the protection of women and children, including data on violence against women and children;
i. establish and develop a Child-Friendly Regency;

Article 34

(1) The Regional Government encourages business entities to participate in protection of women and children.

(2) Participation as referred to in paragraph (1) may be in the form of: a. socialization to employees within the company regarding the rights of women and children;

b. socialization to employees within the company regarding the dangers of acts of violence against women and children;

c. providing assistance in the implementation of protection of women and children as a form of corporate social responsibility.

Article 25

(1) Regional Government is obligated and responsible for organizing special protection of children.

(2) Special protection of children as referred to in paragraph (1) is provided to:

a. children in emergency situations;
b. children in conflict with the law;
c. children from minority and isolated groups;
d. children who are exploited economically and/or sexually;
e. children who are victims of narcotics, alcohol, psychotropic, and other addictive substance abuse;
f. children who are victims of pornography;
g. children with HIV/AIDS;
h. child victims of abduction, sale, and/or trafficking; i. child victims of physical and/or psychological violence;
j. child victims of sexual crimes;
k. child victims of terrorist networks;
l. children with disabilities;
m. child victims of maltreatment and neglect;
n. children with deviant social behavior; and
o. children who are victims of stigmatization from labeling related to their parents' conditions.
(3) Provisions regarding procedures for special protection of children as referred to in paragraph (2) shall be further regulated by Regent Regulation.

Article 16

Every woman and child victim of violence has the right to receive: a. protection;

b. information;

c. excellent service;

d. continuous treatment up to the rehabilitation stage;

e. confidential treatment;

f. psychological and legal assistance; and

g. guarantee of rights related to their status as family members and members of society.

Article 2

The implementation of protection of women and children is based on the principles of: a. respect and fulfillment of the rights of children and women; b. justice and gender equality;

c. non-discrimination;

d. the best interests of women and children victims of violence; and e. legal certainty.

Article 17

In addition to receiving the rights as referred to in Article 16, child victims of violence also receive special rights in the form of:

a. the right to respect and full use for survival, growth and development;
b. the right to basic services;
c. the right to equal protection;
d. the right to be free from various stigmas; and
e. the right to freedom.

Article 13

Sexual violence as referred to in Article 10 letter c is caused by:

a. acts in the form of sexual harassment;
b. forced sexual intercourse;
c. forced sexual intercourse in an unnatural or unwanted manner; and/or d. forced sexual intercourse with another person for commercial purposes and or certain purposes.

Article 10

Violence against women and children can be in the form of:

a. physical violence;
b. psychological violence;
c. sexual violence;
d. neglect; and
e. exploitation.

