(1) In the implementation of protection of women and children, the Regional Government may conduct cooperation with:

a. other regional governments;
b. government commissions/bodies/agencies in the field of protection of women and children;
c. police;
d. advocates;
e. health workers;
f. social workers;
g. companion volunteers;
h. spiritual advisors;
i. non-governmental organizations engaged in the field of protection of women and children; and
j. the community.
(2) Cooperation as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be in the form of:
a. socialization;
b. provision of legal aid;
c. health services;
d. victim assistance;
e. counseling;
f. social rehabilitation;
g. spiritual guidance;
h. repatriation and social reintegration;
i. Family Welfare Consultation Institution (LK3).
(3) Cooperation as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be implemented in accordance with laws and regulations.

Article 23

(1) Services as referred to in Article 21 letter b are organized through activities:

a. complaint services;
b. health services;
c. counseling;
d. spiritual guidance;
e. social services;
f. legal aid and assistance services; and/or
g. repatriation and reintegration.
(2) Services as referred to in paragraph (1) are organized with: a. quick and appropriate;
b. safe and comfortable;
c. sense of empathy;
d. non-discrimination;
e. easily accessible;
f. free of charge; and
g. guaranteed confidentiality.
(3) Services as referred to in paragraph (1) are organized in an integrated manner by PPT.

Article 16

Every woman and child victim of violence has the right to receive: a. protection;

b. information;

c. excellent service;

d. continuous treatment up to the rehabilitation stage;

e. confidential treatment;

f. psychological and legal assistance; and

g. guarantee of rights related to their status as family members and members of society.

Article 17

In addition to receiving the rights as referred to in Article 16, child victims of violence also receive special rights in the form of:

a. the right to respect and full use for survival, growth and development;
b. the right to basic services;
c. the right to equal protection;
d. the right to be free from various stigmas; and
e. the right to freedom.

Article 3

The implementation of protection of women and children aims to: a. ensure the fulfillment of the rights of women and children so that they can participate optimally in society in accordance with human dignity; b. provide protection and provide a sense of security to women and children from all forms of violence and discrimination;

c. prevent and eliminate all forms of violence and exploitation against women and children including trafficking in persons;

d. provide services to women and children victims of violence, reporters, and witnesses; and

e. empower women and children victims of violence.

Article 41

Every person is prohibited from:

a. treating women and children in a discriminatory manner which results in women and children experiencing loss, both material and moral, thus impeding their social function;
b. treating women and children with disabilities in a discriminatory manner;
c. placing, allowing, involving, ordering the involvement of children in situations of maltreatment and neglect;
d. placing, allowing, committing, ordering to commit or participating in committing violence against women and children;
e. committing violence or threats of violence to force women and children to have sexual intercourse with him or with another person;
f. committing violence or threats of violence, forcing, committing fraud, committing a series of lies, or persuading women and children to commit or allow obscene acts to be committed;
g. placing, allowing, committing, ordering to commit or participating in committing kidnapping, sale and/or trafficking of women and children; h. obstructing women and children from enjoying their own culture, acknowledging and practicing the teachings of their religion and/or using their own language without neglecting access to community and cultural development;
i. placing, allowing, committing, ordering to commit or participating in committing economic and/or sexual exploitation of women and children;
j. intentionally placing, allowing, involving women and children in the abuse, production and distribution of narcotics and/or psychotropic substances and/or
k. intentionally placing, allowing, involving, ordering the involvement of women and children in the abuse, production and distribution of alcohol and other addictive substances.

Article 32

(1) The community may participate in the implementation of protection of women and children.

(2) Community participation as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be in the form of: a. establishing family partners at the sub-district/village level;

b. establishing non-governmental organizations whose field of work is in the field of protection of women and children;

c. conducting socialization of women's rights independently;

d. conducting preventive efforts if finding indications of violence against women and children in the community;

e. providing first aid to women and children victims of violence; and

f. reporting to competent authorities if violence against women and children occurs in their environment.

(3) Community participation as referred to in paragraph (1) may be conducted by individuals, non-governmental organizations, educational institutions, religious institutions, and business entities.

